# Part 4: encryption

- **Depends on**: [[HQL-0053|the proposal]] for effects and secret
  values; [[01-bytes|part 1]] for `Bytes`; [[02-keys|part 2]] for the
  key types; [[HQL-0021]] for endpoints, `source`,
  `tap`, `target` and `sink`
- **Needed by**: [[HQL-0054]], whose `decrypt_with` and
  `decrypted_with` take a stored identity

## Scope

Authenticated encryption under a shared key, encryption to the public keys
of recipients, and endpoints that encrypt what a format writes. Python's
`cryptography.hazmat.primitives.ciphers.aead` and the `age` command line and
library cover the same ground.

Encryption is two things here, and neither is a `Format`. Over a value it is
an operation from `Bytes` to `Bytes`. Over an endpoint it is a layer between
the format and the transport: the format writes a document's octets, the
layer encrypts them, and the transport stores the result. A format describes
a representation ([[07-formats-and-codecs|HQL-0024 part 7]]);
`orders.jsonl.age` is JSON Lines, encrypted.

## Shared-key encryption

```hql
// lib/std/crypto/mod.hql
/// The authenticated encryption of `self` under `key`, with `aad` bound to
/// it and not encrypted: a new 12-octet nonce, then the ciphertext, then the
/// 16-octet tag.
effectful fn encrypt<C : Cipher>(self: Bytes, key: SecretKey<C>, *, aad: Bytes = Bytes("")) : Bytes
/// The encryption of the UTF-8 octets of `self`.
effectful fn encrypt<C : Cipher>(self: String, key: SecretKey<C>, *, aad: Bytes = Bytes("")) : Bytes
/// The plaintext of `self` under `key` and `aad`, or `none` where `self` is
/// not an encryption of anything under them.
fn decrypt<C : Cipher>(self: Bytes, key: SecretKey<C>, *, aad: Bytes = Bytes("")) : Option<Bytes>
```

```text
nonce (12 octets) || ciphertext (len(plaintext) octets) || tag (16 octets)
```

- `encrypt` MUST draw the nonce from the host generator on every call.
  Encryptions of one plaintext under one key therefore differ.
- `decrypt` MUST give `none` for input shorter than 28 octets, a changed
  octet anywhere, a wrong key and a wrong `aad`, and MUST NOT give any
  plaintext before the tag is checked.
- The reference page of `encrypt` MUST state the bound of 2^32 encryptions
  per key under random nonces, for both ciphers.

## Encryption to recipients

A message for a public key is an age v1 file: a header with one stanza per
recipient, then the payload in 64 KiB chunks under ChaCha20-Poly1305
(age-encryption.org/v1). The `age` command line reads and writes the same
octets.

```hql
/// The age encryption of `self` to every key of `recipients`: any one of
/// their private keys decrypts it.
effectful fn encrypt_to(self: Bytes, recipients: List<PublicKey<X25519>>) : Bytes
effectful fn encrypt_to(self: String, recipients: List<PublicKey<X25519>>) : Bytes
/// The plaintext of the age message `self` for `identity`, or `none` where
/// it is not a message to that key or has been changed.
fn decrypt_with(self: Bytes, identity: PrivateKey<X25519>) : Option<Bytes>

/// The age encryption of `self` under a passphrase, by age's scrypt
/// recipient with work factor 18.
effectful fn encrypt_with_passphrase(self: Bytes, passphrase: String) : Bytes
fn decrypt_with_passphrase(self: Bytes, passphrase: String) : Option<Bytes>

/// The ASCII armor of an age message: `-----BEGIN AGE ENCRYPTED FILE-----`.
fn armor(self: Bytes) : String
/// The age message an armored text holds, or `none`.
fn unarmor(self: String) : Option<Bytes>
```

- `recipients` MUST NOT be empty; an empty list is an evaluation error.
- `decrypt_with_passphrase` MUST refuse a scrypt work factor above 22 as
  an evaluation error, so a message cannot make a reader spend unbounded
  memory and time.
- An `ssh-ed25519` recipient line is not a recipient here. A program that
  holds an SSH key and wants age encryption generates an `X25519` pair.

## Encrypted endpoints

```hql
/// An endpoint whose writes are age-encrypted to `recipients` and stored in
/// `endpoint`.
type EncryptedEnd : Writable
fn encrypted_to(endpoint: Writable, recipients: List<PublicKey<X25519>>) : EncryptedEnd

/// An endpoint whose contents are those of `endpoint`, age-decrypted with
/// `identity`.
type DecryptedEnd : Replayable
type DecryptedLive : Readable
fn decrypted_with(endpoint: Replayable, identity: PrivateKey<X25519>) : DecryptedEnd
fn decrypted_with(endpoint: Readable, identity: PrivateKey<X25519>) : DecryptedLive
```

- The constructors MUST be `Pure`, as every endpoint constructor is; a
  `target`, `sink`, `source` or `tap` over one is the effect.
- When the format is omitted, a literal path ending in `.age` MUST imply the
  format of the path without `.age`: `file("orders.jsonl.age")` under either
  wrapper is `JsonLines`.
- `target` over an `EncryptedEnd` MUST write one age file, replacing the
  file atomically as HQL-0021 has it for every file. `sink` MUST encrypt
  each chunk as it fills and write the last chunk when the stream ends; an
  interrupted `sink` leaves the file it replaced.
- `encrypted_to(file(p).end, …)` MUST be a check error. An age file is one
  header and one stream of chunks, and nothing is appended to it.
- `source` over a `DecryptedEnd` MUST authenticate every chunk before it
  gives any document. `tap` over either MUST give the documents of a chunk
  once that chunk is authenticated; a chunk that fails, or a file that ends
  without its final chunk, ends the stream with an evaluation error naming
  the endpoint and the octet offset of the chunk.
- An identity that opens no stanza of the header MUST be an evaluation
  error naming the endpoint and the stanza types the header holds, never a
  key.
- The wrappers apply to byte endpoints. `decrypted_with` reads a file,
  standard input or an HTTP resource; `encrypted_to` writes a file or
  standard output, and any other endpoint is an evaluation error naming it.
- A chunk that fails, or a file that ends without its final chunk, is
  named by the octet offset of the chunk in the age file: the payload's
  start, after the header and its 16-octet nonce, plus 65552 octets per
  chunk before it. A message transport, a topic or a subject, carries one value per
  message; a program encrypts each with `encrypt_to` in a `map`.

## Examples

```hql
import crypto
import encoding

// Encrypt one field of each record for the analytics team, and keep the rest.
team = read_public_key(file("team.age.pub"), Age, X25519)
patients
| map(p => {id: p.id, ward: p.ward,
            diagnosis: p.diagnosis.encrypt_to([team]).encode(Base64)})
| sink(file("patients.jsonl"))

// Write an export only its recipients can read, and read it back.
auditor  = read_public_key(file("auditor.age.pub"), Age, X25519)
identity = read_private_key(file("identity.txt"), Age, X25519)
orders | target(encrypted_to(file("orders.jsonl.age"), [team, auditor]))

// In a later evaluation: one evaluation does not read a file it replaced.
source(decrypted_with(file("orders.jsonl.age"), identity))

// A field encrypted under a key the program shares with another service.
key = generate_secret_key(Aes256Gcm)
token.encrypt(key, aad = Bytes(user_id)).encode(Base64Url)
```

Cases: [AEAD round trip](../../../../examples/crypto/queries/aead-round-trip.hql),
[a field for a recipient](../../../../examples/crypto/queries/encrypt-a-field.hql),
[an encrypted file](../../../../examples/crypto/queries/encrypted-file.hql).

## Test plan

- AES-256-GCM against the NIST CAVP vectors and ChaCha20-Poly1305 against
  RFC 8439 section 2.8.2, with a fixed nonce through an internal test
  entry point that programs cannot reach.
- `decrypt` gives `none` for each of: a flipped octet in the nonce, the
  ciphertext and the tag; a truncation; a wrong key; a wrong `aad`.
- age: the test vectors of the age project (`testkit`), a file written by
  the `age` command line read here, and a file written here read by it,
  for X25519 and scrypt recipients; a work factor of 23 refused.
- Encrypted endpoints: a `target` and a `sink` of 200 000 JSON lines read
  back by `source` and by `tap`; a file truncated at a chunk boundary and
  one with a flipped octet in its third chunk, each ending `tap` after the
  documents of the first two chunks with the declared error.
- `encrypted_to(file(p).end, …)` refused at check time.
